E8.text:
\begin{proposition}\label{dis:type-b-reduction}
If $G$ has a bridge of type (b) with at least two interior vertices, then $G\cong\mathfrak{T}_{66}$, or the twins and paths can be chosen as at the start of this section so that one of the following holds.
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\roman*)}]
\item All three paths are edges.
\item There is a bridge of type (c) with at least two interior vertices.
\item Every bridge with nonempty interior has three attachments.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Let $B$ be such a bridge. After interchanging $A_1,A_2$ if necessary, Lemma~\ref{dis:type-b-forms} makes $B$ one of $B_1,\ldots,B_5$ and gives $P_x=cd$. In case (i) of that lemma, the check in~\supp{E8} gives $G\cong\mathfrak{T}_{66}$ (Figure~\ref{fig:type-b-conclusion}, left). We may therefore assume case (ii), so every other $(J_1\cup J_2)$-bridge attaches within $\{c,d,e,f\}$. In particular, $af,eb$ are absent. The simple-face test $H_{\mathrm{simp}}$ for $(P_m;s_x,t_x)$ consists of $P_w$, $cd$, the other bridges and the added edges $ce,df$, and is nonplanar.

E34.text:
\begin{proposition}\label{r1:three-outcome}
If, for every choice of the four twins, each component outside the resulting $\Psi_3$ has exactly three neighbors, then $G$ is isomorphic to one of $\mathfrak T_7,\mathfrak T_{33},\mathfrak T_{35},\mathfrak T_{49},\mathfrak T_{70}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The preceding lemmas give all bridges with nonplanar triangle completion in step (1). Every other bridge with nonempty interior is a three-edge star by Lemma~\ref{lem:flat-replacement}. Two such stars on the same additional triple would give a twin triple meeting $\{a,b,c\}$ or $\{a,d,e\}$ twice, so step (2) includes all remaining bridges. Lemmas~\ref{r1:twin-count} and~\ref{r1:three-attachment-setup} give the five choices in step (3). Thus Construction~\ref{r1:final-construction} includes every remaining obstruction. For $n_c=n_e=2$, it gives $31{,}063$ candidates up to isomorphism fixing $a,b,c,d,e$. Exclude any candidate with a degree-three vertex whose neighbors are not independent, or for which some choice of the four twins gives a component with four or five neighbors. The degree-three vertices in the recorded witnesses lie outside $\{a,b,c,d,e\}$, so increasing $n_c$ or $n_e$ preserves their neighborhoods. The chosen twins therefore still define the same $\Psi_3$, and each witnessed component lies in a component retaining all its neighbors on $\Psi_3$. Both exclusions remain valid for all five choices of $(n_c,n_e)$. The $193$ remaining candidates give $965$ cases over these choices. Of these, $172$ are toroidal and $782$ have a proper nontoroidal minor. The remaining $11$ are copies of the five graphs in the statement~\supp{E34}.

R4.text:
\begin{lemma}\label{r2:cellular-center}
Suppose a graph consists of $\Psi_2$, bridges with three
attachments among $a,b,c,d$, and possibly $cd$, with $cd$
or a bridge joining $c,d$. If it has a torus embedding
in which a closed regular neighborhood of $\Psi_2$ has
genus one, then each added bridge $B$ has a planar
completion $B+xy+xz$ for some labeling of its attachments.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:torus-complement}, every complementary region is a disk. Since $cd$ or a bridge joins $c,d$, some boundary walk contains both. In the genus-one rotations of $\Psi_2$ with such a walk, no two attachment labels alternate in a face containing at least three labels~\supp{R4}.
